13019	{"artifactId":"LLD-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e-s2","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e-s2-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-12T04:32:22.374Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design covers DEF s2 ac1 (only eligible files admitted from a mixed source; exact revisions, attribution and modifications kept) and ac2 (notices for an exported release carry the required license and attribution records), and builds nothing outside sc3.","evidence":"DEF-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e.json:472-490: ac1 'a source with eligible and unknown-license files … only eligible files are admitted and their exact revisions, attribution and modifications are retained'; ac2 'all included third-party material has the required license and attribution records'. The LLD's ImportReport and the reviewed-policy design (§3.2, §3.4) cover ac1. The deterministic notices and the reconstruction check (§3.5) cover ac2. The ImportReport postcondition rules out publication, catalog writes and IPC/MCP surfaces. [files: .insrc/artifacts/DEF-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e.json]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The sc3 interface shapes (SourceFile, SourceManifest, ImportReport) match the approved HLD sketch exactly.","evidence":"HLD-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e.json:283 interfaceSketch is identical field-for-field to LLD §2.1, including eligibility 'eligible' | 'excluded' | 'unresolved', policyVersion and schemaVersion: 1. [files: .insrc/artifacts/HLD-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e.json]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Existing git revParse returns short hashes, and shell-helper decodes output as UTF-8 text, so neither can be used to pin a commit or preserve binary bytes.","evidence":"helpers.ts:40-42 runs `git rev-parse --short`. shell-helper.ts:89-92 does `stdout += chunk.toString('utf8')` and silently drops bytes past maxBytes (it only sets a stdoutTruncated flag). Both points the design makes are correct. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"candidateContentDigest returns '' unless the coverage path appears exactly once. It also excludes the coverage file's digest and reviewDigest, so eligibility can be bound before the coverage envelope is finalized.","evidence":"canonical.ts:96-106: `if (coverage.length !== 1) return ''`, then it deletes releaseId, reviewDigest and coverage[0].digest before hashing. The manifest needs a coverage entry with a path and mediaType, but its bytes and the reviewDigest value do not affect the candidate digest. Source-manifest, notices and lineage file digests do stay in the hash. This confirms the §3.5 assembly order and the 'noncomputable digest → failure' handling. [files: src/design/corpus/canonical.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"checkPublication requires exactly one eligibility assessment, bound to the current candidateContentDigest, with passed=true and no issues.","evidence":"validate.ts:337-345 requires exactly one assessment per stage. It flags assessment.stale when the digest does not match and assessment.failed when !passed or issues is non-empty. STAGE_ASSESSMENT_SCHEMA (contracts.ts:57-60) requires DIGEST_SCHEMA for candidateContentDigest, so an empty digest cannot even be decoded. That justifies the design's 'do not construct a StageAssessment with an empty digest'. [files: src/design/corpus/validate.ts, src/design/corpus/contracts.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"S002 reuses S001's 'portable path rules and NFC/case collision comparisons' and UTC ISO validation (for retrievedAt) as existing helpers.","evidence":"validate.ts:16 exports `isPortablePath`, but `pathCollisionKey` (validate.ts:20, `path.normalize('NFC').toLowerCase()`) and `utcDate` (validate.ts:21) are module-private `const`/`function` with no export. index.ts re-exports only exported symbols. To reuse them as written, S002 must either add exports to S001's validate.ts, which touches the S001 surface the design says it leaves unchanged, or duplicate the logic, which risks drifting from S001's collision and timestamp rules. [files: src/design/corpus/validate.ts, src/design/corpus/index.ts]","action":"State in §3.1 whether S002 adds `pathCollisionKey`/`utcDate` exports to src/design/corpus/validate.ts (and list that as a change site) or duplicates them with a parity test against S001 behaviour.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The ValidationIssue codes the error paths use (provenance.incomplete, reference.missing, digest.mismatch, path.invalid, inventory.invalid, limit.exceeded) already exist in S001, so no S001 schema change is needed.","evidence":"contracts.ts:53-55 ISSUE_CODES includes all six codes. [files: src/design/corpus/contracts.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.3","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"S001 safety limits of <=10,000 files and <=64 MiB exist for reuse.","evidence":"canonical.ts:5 `CORPUS_LIMITS = { recordBytes: 1 MiB, packageBytes: 64 MiB, files: 10_000, depth: 64 }`, exported. [files: src/design/corpus/canonical.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"New sources/* modules can be added without changing S001's public barrel, and the Git reader stays out of the pure src/design/corpus/index.ts.","evidence":"index.ts re-exports only contracts/canonical/validate. module-isolation.test.ts:11-56 compiles the corpus and imports out/design/corpus/index.js with fs/net/child_process forbidden, so a pure-boundary smoke test already has a template to extend. The design names a separate entry for git-reader.ts. [files: src/design/corpus/index.ts, src/design/corpus/__tests__/module-isolation.test.ts]","action":"None. Make sure the pure-boundary test also covers the new pure sources entry (eligibility/notices), not just index.js.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Lineage stored as a separate file (sources/lineage.json) listed in manifest.files is covered by candidate hashing, and S001's release validation accepts it without schema change.","evidence":"canonical.ts:96-105 hashes every manifest.files entry (path, digest, mediaType) except the coverage digest. validate.ts:193-207 accepts any declared regular file and treats files that are neither records nor sourceManifest/notices/coverage as contentPaths. An extra lineage file therefore validates and is bound by the digest. The manifest has no lineagePath field, so S002 has to find it at the fixed path, as the design states. [files: src/design/corpus/canonical.ts, src/design/corpus/validate.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.2","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Package paths derived from upstream repository paths can be validated with S001's portable path rule.","evidence":"isPortablePath (validate.ts:16-19) rejects '\\\\', '%', ':' and control characters. Real upstream paths can contain '%' or ':', and the design applies the S001 rule to SourceFile.path. This rejects them as path.invalid rather than marking them unresolved, which fits 'unsafe paths → abort request'. However, one such path aborts the whole mixed import. I could not tell from the design whether that is intended (§5 makes the unsafe-path case request-level) or whether such files should be reported per file. [files: src/design/corpus/validate.ts]","action":"Clarify whether a non-portable upstream path fails the whole request or only that file.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"S002 does no catalog/package installation (S004), coverage (S003/sc4) or publication (S005). It produces only the 'eligibility' stage and does not change sc1/sc2.","evidence":"HLD:274-282 sc3 is ownedByStory s2. The LLD keeps staging in memory ('durable catalog/package installation remains S004'), emits stage='eligibility' only, and says 'No changes to S001 public schema'. S001's LLD (LLD-…-s1.json:414) defines a dependency-inverted assessment input for downstream eligibility, which this matches. The only boundary pressure is the unexported-helper issue raised under new-versus-reuse. [files: .insrc/artifacts/HLD-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e.json, .insrc/artifacts/LLD-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e-s1.json]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Each failure is detectable and its handling is stated consistently. In particular, a digest mismatch is handled the same way across the error-path list.","evidence":"§5 case 1 says 'mismatched evidence digests' → mark the path unresolved (per file, other files still admitted). §5 case 3 says 'mismatched source bytes' → 'Request-level ValidationResult failure … no partial admitted result'. §3.3 also fails the whole request on 'digest mismatch'. With ac1 (DEF:475-477) requiring a mixed source to still admit eligible files, an implementer cannot tell whether a stale reviewed-profile digest for one file should abort the whole import or leave that file unresolved. The two choices give different ImportReports and different test expectations. [files: .insrc/artifacts/DEF-7e0ae92e8f3d3c3e.json]","action":"Define one rule: for example, a stale profile digest for one file → that file unresolved, while a Git object or framing integrity failure → request-level failure. Align §3.3, §5 and the mixed-eligibility and reader-safety tests with it.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each acceptance criterion maps to concrete tests, and the existing test framework is node:test plus node:assert/strict via tsx.","evidence":"The acceptance mapping table maps ac1 to source-schema, mixed-eligibility, pinned-reader and others, and ac2 to notice-closure, lineage-closure, candidate-roundtrip and others. The existing corpus tests use `import { test } from 'node:test'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'` (module-isolation.test.ts:2-3), matching the stated framework. [files: src/design/corpus/__tests__/module-isolation.test.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":2,"low":12,"unverified":1},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-12T04:32:22.374Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{"new-versus-reuse.1":{"findingId":"new-versus-reuse.1","status":"resolved","note":"Specified S002-local collision/timestamp primitives and parity matrix against S001 public behavior; only isPortablePath is reused as an export. No S001 surface change.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-12T04:35:01.268Z"},"error-paths.1":{"findingId":"error-paths.1","status":"resolved","note":"Aligned acquisition and eligibility: stale reviewed file/evidence digests yield per-file unresolved outcomes propagated to dependents; Git acquisition-integrity failures abort the request. Updated data model, error paths and test subjects.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-12T04:35:01.272Z"},"data-compatibility.2":{"findingId":"data-compatibility.2","status":"resolved","note":"Made strict v1 selected-path grammar explicit: any requested non-portable path aborts before I/O, while unselected paths have no effect. Added edge case and reader-safety test requirement.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-12T04:35:01.273Z"}}}}}}
